  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-347435",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]={\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-5,$ we evaluate each indicator function:\n\n- $1[x\\ge 0]=1[-5\\ge 0]=0$ (since $-5\\ge 0$ is false)\n\n- $1[-3\\le x<0]=1[-3\\le -5<0]=0$ (since $-3\\le -5$ is false)\n\n- $1[x<-3]=1[-5<-3]=1$ (since $-5<-3$ is true)\n\nSubstituting into the expression for $p(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(-5) & =0\\cdot 25+0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2(-5)) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the indicator functions act as a \"switch\": since $x=-5<-3,$ only the third term contributes to the result.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
